# APP IDENTITY
Name: Mochi (store title "Mochi Reader"). Application ID: app.mochireader.reader. Brand feel: soft, round, friendly, calm. Default light theme: Sakura Milk. Default dark theme: Midnight Sakura. Design an original icon concept (a simple rounded rice-cake silhouette with a small bookmark ribbon, no copyrighted characters) and a few alternate pastel launcher icons. Use "Mochi" consistently in strings, onboarding and the privacy screen.

# PRODUCT VISION AND PRINCIPLES
A very advanced reader with a very minimal interface. The default experience is calm and simple (open a book, read, highlight). Depth is one tap away behind progressive disclosure ("Advanced" expanders). It should feel as simple and polished as Apple Books, look like a modern Liquid Glass interface on Material 3 Expressive, and have the page turning, bookshelf feel and control depth of Moon+ Reader, without the option overload of Librera.
- Content first: while reading, only the text shows. Chrome appears on a center tap and auto-hides.
- NEVER modify the user's original files. Ever.
- 100% offline: every feature works with no network. Privacy by default: no ads, no analytics, no trackers, no account.
- Everything configurable has a sensible default and a one-tap reset. Undo/redo everywhere.
- 60fps minimum, 120fps on capable displays. Page turns never stutter.
- Imitate interaction patterns and feel only. Do NOT use Apple or Moon+ names, logos, icons, textures, sounds, screenshots or fonts (no SF fonts). Use open-licensed fonts (Inter for UI) and open icons (Material Symbols or Phosphor).

# DESIGN LANGUAGE: MATERIAL 3 EXPRESSIVE + LIQUID GLASS + BOOKS-STYLE SIMPLICITY
Each has one job so they do not fight:
- Material 3 Expressive = structure: MaterialExpressiveTheme with MotionScheme.expressive() and the motion tokens for all animation, MaterialShapes and shape morphing (for example the book cover morphing into the reader as a shared-element transition), expressive components (HorizontalFloatingToolbar for the reader and selection toolbars, connected button groups/segmented buttons, FAB menu, wavy progress and loading indicators), and emphasized typography. These APIs were experimental/opt-in when I last checked: verify the current stable status and versions. If something is unavailable, fall back to standard Material 3 with custom spring animations, behind a thin design-system layer.
- Liquid Glass = material for floating chrome only (reader bars, scrubber, bottom nav, sheets, toolbars, popovers; never the book text): translucent container fill with background blur, a tint pulled from the content behind, a thin 1dp luminous border (brighter on top), a soft shadow, capsule or very rounded shapes with concentric radii, controls floating with margins. Blur via RenderEffect on API 31+, an optional AGSL lens/highlight effect on API 33+, and on API 26-30 a semi-opaque tonal surface with the same border and shadow. Max 2 glass layers on screen, capped blur, and freeze the blur during page-turn animations. Provide "Solid chrome" (reduce transparency) and "Reduce motion" toggles, guarantee text contrast on glass with an adaptive scrim, and run an automated contrast check across all themes.
- Apple Books restraint: "Reading Now" home (large current-book card, Continue shelf, Want to Read shelf, Collections); reader with generous margins; a calm progress line ("Page 42 of 310 · 12 pages left in chapter"); a scrubber with a floating chapter/page preview bubble; an "Aa" glass sheet with A-/A+, font previews, circular theme swatches, a page-turn selector (Slide, Curl, Scroll) and brightness, with everything else under "More options" (changes apply live behind the sheet); a floating selection toolbar; pull-down in-context search; light haptics; no badges or nags.

# TECH STACK
Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Material 3 (Expressive APIs, see above), Navigation Compose, min SDK 26, target the latest stable SDK. Room (SQLite, with FTS for search), DataStore, Coroutines/Flow, a lightweight DI choice (Hilt or Koin, justify it), Coil, WorkManager, Baseline Profiles and macrobenchmark scaffolding.
