# LIBRARY AND BOOKSHELF
- Add one or more root folders; scan recursively in the background and detect changes.
- Three views via one segmented control: Shelf, Folders (mirrors the real directory tree with breadcrumbs: this is the "folder-wise" view), Collections (user collections, tags, automatic series).
- Shelf styles (a setting; default is the flat grid): (1) Wooden shelf in the spirit of classic iBooks/Moon+: books standing on planks with a front ledge, contact shadows and subtle spine depth, wood generated procedurally or from an open-licensed texture and tinted by theme shelf tokens, plus an optional user shelf background image with a dim/blur slider; (2) Flat grid with large covers, soft shadows and a progress ring; (3) List with small cover, title, author and a progress bar.
- Long-press to drag, reorder and drop a book onto another to make a stack. Optional spine thickness by page count. Generated covers (title on a themed gradient) for books without covers. Cover-to-reader morph transition. Pull down to rescan. A large-title header that collapses on scroll.
- Sort by title, author, last read, date added, progress, size. Filter by format, status (unread, reading, finished) and tag. Fast search over title, author and optionally annotation text. Batch actions (collection, mark finished, hide, remove from library). Never delete files unless the user explicitly chooses "delete file" with a confirmation. Cover extraction cached off the main thread. Duplicate detection by content hash with merge/hide.
- Must stay smooth with 5,000 books and with 500 MB PDFs.

# READER: PAGE TURNING, LAYOUT, CONTROLS
Interactive page curl (a real effect, not a canned animation):
- The page follows the finger from any corner or edge. The fold line, curl radius and shadow update every frame. Render the current and next pages as pre-rendered bitmaps onto a GPU mesh or shader (OpenGL ES, or AGSL on API 33+, with a Canvas/mesh fallback). Draw the curled front, the page back (mirrored, slightly faded real text), a dynamic shadow on the page below, and a soft specular highlight along the fold. Physics: velocity-based completion or cancel, inertia, spring settle, tap to turn. Settings: curl strength, shadow strength, show page back, speed, corner vs. edge grab.
- Other modes on the same gesture engine: slide, cover-flip, stack, fade, vertical scroll, none. All honor Reduce motion. Never run layout during a gesture, pool hardware bitmaps, pre-render neighbors, include a jank benchmark.
Layout: font family, size, line height, paragraph spacing, per-side margins, alignment (left or justify), first-line indent, hyphenation (Knuth-Liang with bundled patterns), single/dual page (auto dual on tablets, foldables and landscape), columns, orientation lock. Bundle open-licensed fonts (a long-reading serif, a sans, a dyslexia-friendly font, a mono, and a few handwriting fonts) and allow importing TTF/OTF. Reading position is a locator (progression, chapter, text-quote anchor), so positions and highlights survive layout changes. Optional reading ruler/focus line and an optional themed mini status bar.
Controls (Moon+-style depth, under "Advanced"): configurable tap zones with presets (Classic, Left-handed, Scroll); an action map assigning actions (next/prev, chapter jump, toggle theme, brightness, TTS, bookmark, search, undo jump) to taps, swipes, long-press and volume keys; five auto-scroll modes (rolling blind, by pixel, by line, by page, continuous) with real-time speed; left-edge swipe for brightness.

# PDF AND REFLOW
Two modes with a one-tap toggle. (1) Fixed layout: smooth pinch-zoom and pan (zoom up to at least 1000%), fit-width/fit-page, auto margin crop, smart double-tap zoom into the text column. (2) Reflow: extract positioned text with PDFium and rebuild flowing text. Implement explicitly: line grouping by baseline and font metrics, multi-column detection with recursive XY-cut, reading-order reconstruction, paragraph detection by line gap and indent, dehyphenation, repeated header/footer/page-number removal, footnote and caption detection, figures and tables kept as inline images, results cached per file and computed lazily in the background, and a fallback to fixed layout for low-confidence pages with a small notice. Unit-test the column detector and dehyphenation.

# ANNOTATIONS (non-destructive, a hard requirement)
- The original file is NEVER written to. All annotations live in Room.
- Basic types: highlight (several colors, plus style variants: underline, squiggly, strikethrough), notes attached to a selection (Markdown, see below), named bookmarks, freehand ink (pen, highlighter-pen, eraser; stylus pressure and tilt, palm rejection, optional finger drawing).
- Anchoring: EPUB/text via locator + text-quote selector (prefix, exact, suffix) + progression; PDF via page index + normalized quad coordinates (0..1) + text quote; ink and shapes as vector paths in normalized page coordinates on an overlay layer (crisp at any zoom).
- Selection UX: long-press, drag handles, a floating glass toolbar (colors, note, copy, define, translate-via-intent, search, share). Also a "quick highlight" mode where dragging across text highlights instantly. Tapping a highlight opens a popover (color, note, delete).
- Annotations panel per book (by chapter; filter by color/type; tap to jump) and a global "All notes" screen with search.
- Highlight colors map to theme roles so they stay readable in every theme, and a color-blind-safe palette option exists.

## ANNOTATION STUDIO (vector markup with CAD-style precision)
A drawing mode (a floating glass toolbar) for PDFs, comics and fixed pages:
- Tools: pen, highlighter, line, polyline, ARROW (single/double head, several head styles), rectangle, rounded rectangle, ellipse/circle, polygon, revision cloud, callout/text box, dimension and measure (distance, angle, polyline length, area), stamp/sticker, spotlight/cutout, stroke eraser and object eraser, lasso/box select.
- Precision: snapping (grid, endpoints, midpoints, centers, intersections, text-line boundaries, page edges) with an on/off toggle; ortho/polar constraint (hold to lock to 0/45/90 degrees, or 15-degree steps); a magnifier loupe and an offset "precision pointer" so a finger does not cover the target; stylus hover preview; a properties panel with numeric entry for position, length, angle, width, height and stroke width.
- Objects: every markup is an object with handles for move, resize and rotate; multi-select; group/ungroup; align/distribute; duplicate; copy/paste across pages; z-order; lock; named layers (visibility, lock, color) such as "My notes" and "Review"; line styles (dash, dot, arrowheads), fill, opacity; style presets and recent styles; unlimited undo/redo per book.
- Measuring: calibrate a scale on a page (draw a line, enter a real distance), units (mm, cm, in, ft, px), and measurement objects persist with their scale.
- Command palette (type to run): for example "line", "arrow", "undo", "layer review", "snap off", "theme nord", "go to 45", "highlight yellow". The same palette is available in the reader (search all actions and settings).
- Export without touching the source: a flattened or standard-annotation PDF COPY (never overwrite), SVG export of the markup layer, and a JSON backup.
- Reflowable EPUB/MD text can have shapes anchored to a text range or placed in a "page snapshot" mode. State this limitation clearly in the UI.

